## 3. The Software & Processing Pipeline
When an RGB camera captures this feature under overhead light, it sees a bright white shape on a dark background (the inverse of traditional paper-printed markers).
## The Low-Overhead Algorithm Rules:

   1. Frame Inversion: The software instantly flips the grayscale image matrix natively using cv2.threshold with THRESH_BINARY_INV or cv2.bitwise_not(). This transforms the specular glossy flash into an explicit white binary mask.
   2. Elimination of Contours: Instead of processing heavy, CPU-intensive geometric contour maps (findContours) or shape-matching metrics (matchShapes), the system utilizes Connected Components with Stats (connectedComponentsWithStats). This scans the pixels sequentially in a single pass to map groups, instantly returning bounding boxes and weight centroids via hardware vectors.
   3. Region of Interest (ROI) Slicing:
   * Search Mode (Frame 1): Bypasses cropping and scans the entire frame to acquire the initial lock.
      * Track Mode (Frame 2+): Creates a tightly cropped pixel window around the last known coordinates of the target. This shrinks the processed search window by over 95% (e.g., from 307,200 pixels down to under 14,000 pixels), preserving CPU cache lines and minimizing execution latency to <0.4ms.
   
